WEBVTT

00:00:06.095 --> 00:00:08.153
A sundial turns space into time.

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:11.875
Sunlight and the geometry of its
gnomon decide where the shadow falls,

00:00:11.915 --> 00:00:13.359
and when it reaches each mark.

00:00:13.891 --> 00:00:16.987
Gnomon4D brings this idea to
robot world-action models:

00:00:17.027 --> 00:00:18.929
learn where, and when, to focus.

00:00:22.361 --> 00:00:24.062
Give a robot an instruction.

00:00:24.588 --> 00:00:26.713
A world-action model imagines the future,

00:00:26.753 --> 00:00:28.867
then generates the actions
that make it real.

00:00:31.328 --> 00:00:32.806
But two gaps remain.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.412
First, what should the future encode?

00:00:35.943 --> 00:00:38.425
RGB mixes motion with texture and light.

00:00:38.957 --> 00:00:41.595
Depth adds geometry, but
one frame at a time.

00:00:42.126 --> 00:00:44.006
Neither says which point went where.

00:00:46.061 --> 00:00:48.264
Recent models do bring in geometry.

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:51.574
Some predict depth with
the video, frame by frame.

00:00:52.116 --> 00:00:55.603
Some learn from 4D tracks in training,
then drop them at inference.

00:00:56.123 --> 00:01:00.078
Others predict sparse 3D traces, and
need a separate interface to act.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:04.018
Geometry helps, but the action
never reads explicit motion.

00:01:06.661 --> 00:01:08.753
Second, where does the action look?

00:01:09.279 --> 00:01:12.587
Probing released world-action models,
we find their attention spread over the

00:01:12.627 --> 00:01:14.997
arm, up to two point three times uniform.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.078
The interaction region, where
the gripper meets the object,

00:01:18.118 --> 00:01:21.203
matters most for control, yet
gets at most one point five.

00:01:24.161 --> 00:01:27.324
Gnomon4D closes both gaps
with three contributions.

00:01:27.839 --> 00:01:30.645
GeoDelta, an explicit 4D motion field.

00:01:31.187 --> 00:01:34.919
InSAttn and TransAgg, which tell
the action where and when to focus.

00:01:35.451 --> 00:01:37.643
And one motion space
for humans and robots,

00:01:37.683 --> 00:01:40.522
for pretraining on five
thousand hours of human video.

00:01:43.361 --> 00:01:44.873
Three experts denoise together:

00:01:44.913 --> 00:01:47.406
future video, GeoDelta, and actions.

00:01:47.943 --> 00:01:51.117
GeoDelta and actions run on a
clock twice as fast as video,

00:01:51.157 --> 00:01:53.070
so the actions are ready halfway through.

00:01:55.595 --> 00:01:56.905
Contribution one.

00:01:57.431 --> 00:01:58.596
GeoDelta.

00:02:00.628 --> 00:02:03.512
GeoDelta follows each point
on the arm and the object.

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:06.475
Lift it to 3D, find it four steps later,

00:02:06.515 --> 00:02:08.239
and project both into the camera.

00:02:08.781 --> 00:02:11.699
The difference, delta u,
delta v, and delta d,

00:02:11.739 --> 00:02:12.681
is its motion.

00:02:13.223 --> 00:02:16.040
One definition holds in
simulation, in VLABench,

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:17.357
and on a real robot.

00:02:19.595 --> 00:02:21.742
A signed mu-law turns
this motion into color,

00:02:21.782 --> 00:02:23.941
so the frozen video
autoencoder encodes it,

00:02:23.981 --> 00:02:25.827
pixel-aligned with the future video.

00:02:28.561 --> 00:02:29.916
Contribution two.

00:02:30.442 --> 00:02:32.322
Where, and when, to focus.

00:02:34.361 --> 00:02:35.739
We compare motions.

00:02:36.253 --> 00:02:38.869
As the gripper approaches,
the block stays still.

00:02:39.401 --> 00:02:42.619
Their motions differ, so the
relative-motion field c is high.

00:02:43.151 --> 00:02:46.235
Once grasped, they move
together, and c drops to zero.

00:02:46.767 --> 00:02:50.499
InSAttn, Interaction-Salient
Attention, reads where c is high.

00:02:52.728 --> 00:02:53.904
Then, time.

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.064
A grasp switches c off.

00:02:56.607 --> 00:02:58.296
A release switches it back on.

00:02:58.828 --> 00:03:01.087
TransAgg, Transition-Anchored Aggregation,

00:03:01.127 --> 00:03:02.961
anchors the readout on these moments.

00:03:05.395 --> 00:03:06.616
Attention flips.

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:10.316
The interaction region rises to
two point four times uniform.

00:03:10.858 --> 00:03:13.229
The rest of the arm falls below uniform.

00:03:15.961 --> 00:03:17.361
Contribution three.

00:03:17.887 --> 00:03:20.458
One motion space, for humans and robots.

00:03:22.495 --> 00:03:26.338
GeoDelta describes how surfaces
move, not how a body is controlled.

00:03:26.875 --> 00:03:29.704
So human hands and robot
grippers share one motion space,

00:03:29.744 --> 00:03:30.864
without retargeting.

00:03:31.407 --> 00:03:35.853
We pretrain on EgoManip-5000H, five
thousand hours of action-free human video,

00:03:35.893 --> 00:03:38.465
then mid-train on thirty
hours of real-robot data.

00:03:41.128 --> 00:03:45.050
EgoManip-5000H starts from
thirty-seven thousand hours of video.

00:03:45.587 --> 00:03:48.147
We keep real scenes, then
egocentric manipulation,

00:03:48.187 --> 00:03:51.897
then clips with hands in view, and
render hand GeoDelta from fitted meshes.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.743
It spans vehicles, electronics, kitchens,

00:03:54.783 --> 00:03:56.339
crafts and the outdoors.

00:03:59.061 --> 00:04:00.606
Now, the results.

00:04:05.461 --> 00:04:08.479
Gnomon4D leads all three
simulation benchmarks.

00:04:09.005 --> 00:04:13.418
On RoboTwin 2.0, it averages ninety-four
point three percent over fifty bimanual

00:04:13.458 --> 00:04:14.199
tasks.

00:04:16.661 --> 00:04:20.505
On VLABench, its lead over OpenWAM-Alpha
grows from zero point six points in

00:04:20.545 --> 00:04:23.407
distribution to seven point
four under unseen textures.

00:04:25.861 --> 00:04:29.036
On RoboDojo, it doubles the
success rate of OpenWAM-Alpha,

00:04:29.076 --> 00:04:31.937
and reaches twenty-eight point
two percent on precision tasks.

00:04:37.395 --> 00:04:38.861
On a real UR5e arm:

00:04:38.901 --> 00:04:41.361
five tasks, eighty trials
per task for each method,

00:04:41.401 --> 00:04:43.348
half under unseen lighting, tablecloths,

00:04:43.388 --> 00:04:45.301
object appearance, or distractors.

00:04:47.728 --> 00:04:49.909
Gnomon4D ranks first on every task:

00:04:49.949 --> 00:04:51.929
eighty-two point five
percent in distribution,

00:04:51.969 --> 00:04:53.703
seventy-eight under visual shifts.

00:04:54.229 --> 00:04:58.017
Its lead over the strongest baseline
widens from seven points to sixteen.

00:05:00.661 --> 00:05:02.340
Ablations trace each gain.

00:05:02.877 --> 00:05:04.812
Object motion adds three
point four points,

00:05:04.852 --> 00:05:07.893
guided readout two point six, and
pretraining four point eight.

00:05:11.128 --> 00:05:13.945
A sundial reads time from
the geometry of a shadow.

00:05:14.482 --> 00:05:18.024
Gnomon4D reads where and when to
act from the geometry of motion.

00:05:22.595 --> 00:05:23.704
More cases.
